# ML Metrics — Plain Language Standards

Metrics such as precision, recall, F1, TP, FP, and FN are not understood by most readers.
Any table or section that displays these figures MUST include a plain-English explanation
rendered directly in the UI — not in a tooltip, not in a footnote formula.

## Required column header names

| Technical term      | Use instead              |
|---------------------|--------------------------|
| Support (n)         | Cases reviewed           |
| Precision           | Precision                |
| Recall / Sensitivity| Recall (sensitivity)     |
| F1                  | F1 score                 |
| TP                  | Correct                  |
| FP                  | False alarms             |
| FN                  | Missed                   |

## Explanation block

Place a `MetricExplanationBox` (or equivalent) **above** the first metric table in a section.
It must cover every column in plain English. Example wording:

- **Cases reviewed** — how many encounters of this type were in the test set.
- **Precision** — of all the times the model predicted this category, how often was it right. 100% means no false alarms.
- **Recall (sensitivity)** — of all encounters that truly belonged to this category, how many did the model find. 100% means nothing was missed.
- **F1 score** — a single number that balances precision and recall. Higher is better; 100% is perfect.
- **Correct / False alarms / Missed** — counts of right predictions, wrong predictions, and cases the model failed to find.

Do NOT repeat the explanation box for every table in the same section — once per section is enough.

## Scope note

Always clarify that scores cover disagreement cases only:
> "Scores cover disagreement cases only — encounters where the two models gave different answers."

## What NOT to do

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — formula footnote, jargon headers, no explanation
<TableCell>TP</TableCell>
<Typography>Precision = TP/(TP+FP) · F1 = harmonic mean</Typography>

// ✅ GOOD — plain headers + explanation block above the table
<MetricExplanationBox />
<TableCell>Correct</TableCell>
```
